var e={id:`environment`,objectives:[`Explain the formation of acid rain from SO₂ and NOx`,`Describe industrial methods of SO₂ control: FGD, alkaline scrubbing, fluidised-bed combustion`,`Explain how NOx is formed in vehicles and how catalytic converters and SCR control it`,`Compare environmental impacts of different power sources`,`Discuss hazards from natural radioactivity and nuclear waste`,`Outline the waste management hierarchy and the chemistry of landfill and incineration`,`Identify common heavy-metal pollutants and their treatment by reduction and ion exchange`,`Describe acid mine drainage and its remediation`],chapters:[{id:`ev1`,title:`Pollution and Environmental Chemistry — Setting the Scene`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Environmental chemistry studies the sources, behaviour, and removal of substances introduced into the natural environment by human activity. Most pollution comes down to a few well-understood chemical pathways: combustion (CO, CO₂, SO₂, NOx), industrial waste streams (heavy metals, organic solvents), agricultural run-off (nitrates, phosphates, pesticides), and end-of-life products (plastics, electronics). Each of these has both a chemistry of HOW the pollutant enters the environment and a chemistry of WHAT can be done to reduce or remove it.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`POLLUTANT SOURCES & EFFECTS`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[`Source`,`Pollutant`,`Main effect`],rows:[[`coal / oil combustion`,`SO₂`,`acid rain · respiratory irritation`],[`high-T engines`,`NOx`,`acid rain · photochemical smog`],[`incomplete combustion`,`CO`,`toxic · reduces O₂ transport`],[`fossil fuel combustion`,`CO₂`,`greenhouse warming`],[`industry / mining`,`heavy metals`,`persistent toxicity · bioaccumulation`],[`agriculture / sewage`,`NO₃⁻ / PO₄³⁻`,`eutrophication`]],colWidths:[92,92,116],rowH:24}]},caption:`Major pollutant categories in the syllabus`},{type:`h`,text:`Some recurring chemistry ideas`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Acid-base: SO₂ and NOx form acids in moist air → acid rain. Reverse the chemistry with alkaline scrubbers (chapter 3).`,`Redox: NOx and CO are reduced to N₂ and oxidised to CO₂ in catalytic converters (chapter 5). Heavy metals exist in different oxidation states with different toxicities (e.g. Cr(III) vs Cr(VI)).`,`Equilibrium: many pollution-control processes work by shifting an equilibrium — flue gas desulphurisation pulls SO₂ out of the gas phase by reacting it with solid CaCO₃, removing it from the atmospheric cycle.`,`Catalysis: catalytic converters (chapter 5) and selective catalytic reduction (SCR) of NOx in power-station chimneys.`,`Phase equilibria: ion-exchange resins (link to AC-22) take heavy metal ions out of waste water by exchanging them for harmless Na⁺ or H⁺.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why this topic matters`},{type:`ul`,items:[`The chemistry of pollution control is both academically interesting and economically vital — global pollution-control industry is worth ~ $1 trillion/year and growing.`,`Many syllabus topics tie together here: catalysis, equilibria, redox, acid-base, organic toxicology, chromatography for monitoring.`,`Engineers and policy-makers depend on the chemistry to set emissions standards, design equipment, and verify compliance.`,`Public health: respiratory disease, cardiovascular disease, cancer, neurological damage are all linked statistically to specific chemical pollutants.`]},{type:`h`,text:`How the topic is structured`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Chapters 2–3: sulphur dioxide pollution and control.`,`Chapter 4: nitrogen oxides and acid rain.`,`Chapter 5: catalytic converters and lean-burn engines.`,`Chapters 6–7: power generation — fossil fuels and nuclear.`,`Chapter 8: waste management hierarchy.`,`Chapter 9: landfill and incineration in detail.`,`Chapter 10: heavy metals and water-borne pollutants.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`definition`,title:`Pollutant`,body:`A substance present in the environment in higher concentrations than would naturally occur, with measurable detrimental effects on health, ecosystems, or built infrastructure. The same substance (e.g. nitrate) may be a nutrient at low concentration and a pollutant at high concentration.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Environmental chemistry covers both the formation and the abatement of pollutants.`,`Acid-base, redox, equilibrium, and catalysis recur as the underlying chemistry.`,`Topic ties together to most other syllabus areas — kinetics, equilibria, organic, transition metals.`,`Pollution control is a major global industry with measurable public-health benefits.`]}]},{id:`ev2`,title:`Sulphur Dioxide & Acid Rain`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Sulphur dioxide (SO₂) is the most important acidic gas pollutant from coal combustion. Coal contains 0.5 – 4% sulphur (as iron pyrite, FeS₂, or as organic sulphur). When the coal burns, the sulphur oxidises to SO₂, which is released into the flue gas. Acid rain — first identified in the 1970s as the cause of forest decline in Scandinavia — is the chief environmental consequence.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`ACID RAIN · CHEMISTRY`,blocks:[{type:`compare`,left:{title:`SULPHUR PATHWAY`,color:`primary`,body:[`SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃`,`2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃`,`SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄`]},right:{title:`NITROGEN PATHWAY`,color:`accent`,body:[`N₂ + O₂ → 2NO (high T)`,`NO → NO₂ in air`,`NO₂ + H₂O → acids`]}},{type:`note`,text:`Effects: water acidification · forest / stone damage.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`SO₂ → H₂SO₄ in two oxidation steps`},{type:`h`,text:`How SO₂ becomes acid rain`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Step 1: combustion. S(in coal) + O₂ → SO₂(g). Roughly 5–20 kg of SO₂ per tonne of coal burnt.`,`Step 2: oxidation in the atmosphere. 2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃. Slow without catalysts; fast in the presence of NOx (acts as a catalytic carrier of O atoms) or transition-metal aerosols.`,`Step 3: dissolution in water droplets. SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄. Sulphuric acid is a strong acid, fully ionised in water.`,`Step 4: deposition. Acidic rain (pH 4 typical, sometimes pH 3 in heavily polluted areas) falls back onto land and water.`,`Note that SO₂ itself dissolves directly: SO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂SO₃ (sulphurous acid, weak). Both H₂SO₃ and H₂SO₄ contribute, but H₂SO₄ dominates the acidity over time.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Damage caused by acid rain`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Lakes and rivers: pH drops below 5.5 — toxic to fish (e.g. trout); lakes in Sweden and Canada lost most of their aquatic life by the 1980s.`,`Forests: leaches Mg²⁺ and Ca²⁺ from soil (essential nutrients); mobilises Al³⁺ which is toxic to roots; weakens trees against disease and drought.`,`Buildings: limestone/marble (CaCO₃) reacts: CaCO₃ + H₂SO₄ → CaSO₄ + CO₂ + H₂O. Surfaces lose detail; many famous monuments (Acropolis, Taj Mahal, Cologne Cathedral) have suffered visible damage. The CaSO₄ formed is more soluble than the original CaCO₃, so erodes away.`,`Iron/steel structures: H₂SO₄ accelerates rusting. Cathedrals' iron reinforcements; rail bridges; iron statues all corrode faster.`,`Human health: fine sulphate aerosols penetrate deep into lungs; correlated with increased asthma and cardiovascular disease.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Sulphurous gases — a comparison`},{type:`ul`,items:[`SO₂: from coal/oil combustion, ore smelting (Cu, Ni). Major historical pollutant.`,`H₂S: from petroleum refining, sewage, volcanic. Smaller scale. Highly toxic but oxidises quickly to SO₂ in air.`,`COS, CS₂: trace sulphur compounds from natural and industrial sources. Mostly local issues.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Trends in atmospheric SO₂`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Peak SO₂ emissions in industrialised countries: roughly 1970s. EU acid-rain legislation, US Clean Air Act amendments (1990) → emissions cut by 70–90% in subsequent decades.`,`Lakes recovering — but soils are slower to heal because aluminium contamination persists.`,`Developing economies (China, India) had rising emissions through 2000s but China has now begun aggressive control measures and emissions are falling.`,`Continued threat from sulphur-rich coal in some regions; from shipping fuel (heavy fuel oil with up to 4.5% S — mostly capped by IMO sulphur rule from 2020).`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Sulphur in coal — global impact`,body:`A 1 GW coal power station burning 4% sulphur coal at 30% efficiency emits roughly 80 000 tonnes of SO₂ per year if uncontrolled. The same plant with FGD (chapter 3) emits less than 5000 tonnes. The technology exists to cut SO₂ emissions to near-zero — the question is policy and cost.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`SO₂ from coal combustion → atmospheric oxidation → H₂SO₄ → acid rain.`,`Damage: lakes (fish die), forests (nutrient loss), buildings (CaCO₃ erosion).`,`Acid rain pH typically 4, sometimes 3 in heavily polluted areas.`,`Industrialised countries have cut emissions ~80% since the 1970s; trend now global.`]}]},{id:`ev3`,title:`Controlling SO₂ Emissions`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`There are three main industrial strategies for cutting SO₂ emissions, each rooted in acid-base chemistry: flue gas desulphurisation (FGD) with a calcium or alkali sorbent; alkaline scrubbing with NaOH or Mg(OH)₂; and fluidised-bed combustion that captures SO₂ at source. The choice depends on plant size, cost, and the form in which the spent sorbent is disposed.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`SO₂ CONTROL · INDUSTRIAL METHODS`,blocks:[{type:`cards`,cards:[{title:`Wet limestone FGD`,color:`primary`,body:[`spray CaCO₃ slurry`,`CaCO₃ + SO₂ + ½O₂ → CaSO₄ + CO₂`,`>95% removal · gypsum product`]},{title:`Alkaline scrubbing`,color:`accent`,body:[`NaOH / Mg(OH)₂ solution`,`2NaOH + SO₂ → Na₂SO₃ + H₂O`,`effective but reagent costly`]},{title:`Fluidised bed`,color:`green`,body:[`coal + crushed limestone`,`~850 °C combustion`,`captures SO₂ in situ · lower NOx`]}]}],subtitle:`All three remove acidic SO₂ by chemical reaction.`},caption:`Three industrial methods for SO₂ capture`},{type:`h`,text:`Flue Gas Desulphurisation (FGD) — wet limestone`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Most widely used technology — fitted to most modern coal-fired power stations.`,`Process: flue gas is sprayed with a slurry of finely-ground limestone CaCO₃ in water. SO₂ dissolves in the water and reacts with CaCO₃.`,`Net reaction (with oxidation): 2CaCO₃ + 2SO₂ + O₂ → 2CaSO₄ + 2CO₂. The CaSO₄ (gypsum) precipitates out as a solid.`,`Removal efficiency: 90–98% of SO₂ removed.`,`Product: gypsum-grade calcium sulphate is sold for plasterboard manufacture — turns a waste stream into a saleable product.`,`Drawbacks: high water consumption; large installation; CO₂ emitted as a by-product (adding to greenhouse gas emissions).`,`Variant: spray-dry FGD uses a slaked-lime slurry Ca(OH)₂ instead of CaCO₃; the product is a dry powder, easier to handle but lower-value.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Alkaline scrubbing`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Use a strong alkali solution — typically NaOH(aq) or Mg(OH)₂(aq) — to react with SO₂.`,`Reactions: 2NaOH + SO₂ → Na₂SO₃ + H₂O (then partial oxidation: 2Na₂SO₃ + O₂ → 2Na₂SO₄). Or with Mg: Mg(OH)₂ + SO₂ → MgSO₃ + H₂O.`,`Removal efficiency: 95–99% (very high — alkali drives the equilibrium far towards product).`,`Drawback: NaOH is expensive; spent solution is liquid waste containing dissolved salts; suitable for smaller plants where cost per tonne of SO₂ removed is less critical.`,`Used in smelting plants (copper, nickel) where SO₂ concentrations are very high and alkali consumption per kg of fuel is small.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Fluidised-bed combustion (FBC)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Burn the coal in a bed of crushed limestone particles, suspended ("fluidised") by an upward stream of air.`,`CaCO₃ first decomposes thermally: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂.`,`Then captures SO₂ in situ as it forms: CaO + SO₂ + ½O₂ → CaSO₄.`,`Operating temperature is lower than a conventional pulverised-coal boiler (~850 °C vs ~1500 °C). Lower T also REDUCES NOx formation (chapter 4 explains why) — combined air-quality benefit.`,`Removal: 70–90% of SO₂ in situ. Easier to retrofit on smaller boilers than a wet FGD.`,`Used: power station boilers, industrial steam generators, district heating.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Comparing the three methods`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Removal efficiency: alkaline scrubbing > FGD wet > FBC.`,`Capital cost: FGD wet > alkaline scrubbing > FBC.`,`Operating cost: alkaline scrubbing > FGD wet > FBC (NaOH is expensive).`,`Saleable by-product: FGD wet (gypsum) > alkaline scrubbing (Na sulphate) > FBC (CaSO₄ + ash mixed).`,`Co-benefit on NOx: FBC > FGD wet ≈ alkaline scrubbing (FGD reduces NOx slightly via the spray; FBC reduces it via low T).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Other approaches mentioned in passing`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Switch to lower-sulphur coal (varies by mine; Eastern US coal is high-S, Western US low-S).`,`Switch to cleaner fuel: natural gas has essentially no S; oil has moderate S; renewables have zero.`,`Pre-combustion desulphurisation: e.g. coal washing reduces inorganic FeS₂ content but does little for organic S.`,`Selective catalytic reduction (SCR): more often used for NOx but some catalysts also remove SO₂ co-pollutants.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`The unifying chemistry`,body:`Every SO₂ control strategy uses the acid-base reaction of acidic SO₂ with a base — CaCO₃, CaO, NaOH, or Mg(OH)₂. The chemistry is identical to neutralisation in a beaker; only the engineering differs.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`FGD (wet limestone): standard for power stations; gypsum by-product.`,`Alkaline scrubbing: highest removal; expensive; for smelting and small plants.`,`Fluidised-bed combustion: in-situ capture; lower T → also less NOx.`,`All three exploit acid-base reaction of SO₂ with a base.`]}]},{id:`ev4`,title:`Nitrogen Oxides & Acid Rain`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`NOx — a collective term for NO and NO₂ — is the second major contributor to acid rain (after SO₂). Unlike SO₂, NOx is not present in the fuel; it forms by oxidation of atmospheric N₂ at the high temperatures inside engines and combustion chambers. Reducing NOx requires either lowering the combustion temperature (lean-burn engines, low-NOx burners), reducing it back to N₂ after formation (catalytic converters, SCR), or switching to a fuel that burns cooler.`},{type:`h`,text:`How NOx forms`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Three pathways: thermal NOx (the main one), prompt NOx (small fraction), and fuel NOx (only relevant for nitrogen-rich fuels like coal).`,`Thermal NOx: at temperatures above 1500 °C, atmospheric N₂ and O₂ react: N₂ + O₂ → 2NO. The reaction is endothermic by 90.4 kJ/mol — only fast at high T.`,`Inside a car engine cylinder during combustion, T can reach 2500 °C; NOx formation is significant during the few milliseconds of peak combustion.`,`Once formed, NO partially oxidises in the exhaust: 2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂.`,`Atmospheric chemistry: NO + O₃ → NO₂ + O₂; NO₂ photolysis gives back NO + O. Cycle continues until NOx is washed out as nitric acid.`]},{type:`h`,text:`NOx → nitric acid → acid rain`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Slow gas-phase oxidation: 2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂; NO₂ + OH• → HNO₃ (slow, but persistent in atmosphere).`,`Aqueous-phase reaction in cloud droplets: 3NO₂ + H₂O → 2HNO₃ + NO. Net: dissolves out NO₂ as nitric acid, releasing some NO back to the atmosphere.`,`Combined oxidation: 4NO + 3O₂ + 2H₂O → 4HNO₃ (slow but cumulative).`,`Result: rainwater pH drops further. In the polluted northeast US in the 1980s, ~60% of acidity from H₂SO₄ and ~40% from HNO₃; nitric acid contribution rising as SO₂ controls bite.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Other harmful effects of NOx`},{type:`ul`,items:[`PHOTOCHEMICAL SMOG: NOx + sunlight + volatile organic compounds (from vehicle exhaust and industrial solvents) → ground-level ozone (O₃) + peroxyacetyl nitrates (PAN).`,`Ground-level O₃ is a respiratory irritant and a powerful greenhouse gas. PAN is a powerful eye irritant.`,`Smog episodes in Los Angeles (1950s onwards), Mexico City, Beijing, New Delhi, Athens.`,`Health: NOx damages respiratory tissue directly; aggravates asthma; correlated with cardiovascular events.`,`Eutrophication: NOx deposition on land and water adds nitrogen — too much can fertilise algae bloom, killing fish.`,`Greenhouse contribution: N₂O (a related but distinct nitrogen oxide, formed in agriculture and combustion) has 300× the warming effect of CO₂, kg-for-kg. About 6% of total greenhouse gas warming.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Sources of NOx`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Vehicle engines: 50–80% of urban NOx in industrialised countries. Diesel engines are particularly bad (run hotter and leaner than petrol).`,`Power stations and industrial boilers: 20–30%.`,`Aviation: small but rising; high-altitude NOx affects ozone differently than ground-level.`,`Natural sources: biomass burning, soil microbes, lightning. Together ~30% of global NOx but distributed widely.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`definition`,title:`NOx`,body:`Collective term for the gases NO (nitric oxide, colourless) and NO₂ (nitrogen dioxide, brown). N₂O (nitrous oxide) is a related but separate compound, important as a greenhouse gas but not normally counted as "NOx" because it comes from different sources and has different effects.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`NOx forms from atmospheric N₂ at high combustion T (>1500 °C).`,`Atmospheric oxidation → HNO₃ → acid rain (now ~40% of total acidity).`,`Photochemical smog: NOx + sunlight + VOCs → ground-level ozone.`,`Sources: vehicles (urban dominant), power stations, industrial boilers.`]}]},{id:`ev5`,title:`Catalytic Converters & Lean-Burn Engines`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`The two main approaches to reducing vehicle NOx and other exhaust pollutants are: (1) the three-way catalytic converter (chemistry on the exhaust pipe — convert pollutants to harmless gases); and (2) the lean-burn engine (combustion design — produce less pollutant in the first place). The two are complementary; modern petrol engines combine both, and diesels add SCR (selective catalytic reduction with urea injection).`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`THREE-WAY CATALYTIC CONVERTER`,blocks:[{type:`panel`,title:`Catalyst`,body:[`Pt / Pd / Rh on a ceramic honeycomb`,`hot exhaust activates high-area catalyst`],color:`accent`},{type:`compare`,left:{title:`OXIDATION`,color:`green`,body:[`2CO + O₂ → 2CO₂`,`hydrocarbons + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O`]},right:{title:`REDUCTION OF NOx`,color:`pink`,body:[`2NO + 2CO → N₂ + 2CO₂`,`NOx converted to N₂`]}},{type:`note`,text:`Lead poisons the catalyst.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Three-way catalytic converter — handles CO, hydrocarbons, and NOx`},{type:`h`,text:`The three-way catalytic converter — function`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Catalysts: platinum (Pt) and palladium (Pd) for oxidation; rhodium (Rh) for reduction. All three are precious-metal transition elements with active d-orbitals (link to AC-21 transition metal catalysis).`,`Support: ceramic honeycomb (cordierite, 2MgO·2Al₂O₃·5SiO₂) coated with washcoat (γ-Al₂O₃) on which the precious metals are dispersed as nano-particles. The honeycomb maximises surface area while minimising back-pressure.`,`Working temperature: > 350 °C. Below this the catalyst is inactive (the cold-start problem; modern cars use additional electrically-heated catalysts to handle the first 20-30 seconds).`,`Three reactions handled simultaneously: (a) oxidation of CO; (b) oxidation of unburnt hydrocarbons; (c) reduction of NOx.`]},{type:`h`,text:`The three reactions`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Oxidation of CO: 2CO + O₂ → 2CO₂ (over Pt or Pd surface).`,`Oxidation of hydrocarbons: e.g. C₈H₁₈ + 12½O₂ → 8CO₂ + 9H₂O (over Pt or Pd).`,`Reduction of NOx: 2NO + 2CO → N₂ + 2CO₂ (over Rh). The CO is being oxidised AND the NO is being reduced simultaneously — both pollutants destroyed in one elegant step.`,`Optimal performance requires the air/fuel ratio to be near stoichiometric (~14.7:1 by mass, λ ≈ 1.0). Lambda sensors in the exhaust monitor O₂ and the engine ECU adjusts fuel injection in real time to keep the catalyst within its window.`,`Removal efficiency: 90–98% for each pollutant in normal driving conditions.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why catalysts work — recap of AC-21 ideas`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Heterogeneous catalysis: gas-phase reactants adsorb on the metal surface, weakening their bonds and bringing them close together for reaction.`,`Pt, Pd, Rh have partially-filled d-orbitals that interact effectively with both CO (via the carbon lone pair and π* orbital) and NO (via the N lone pair).`,`Catalysts are not consumed — they cycle through adsorbed states and regenerate as products desorb.`,`Catalyst poisons: lead destroys the catalyst (forms stable Pt-Pb compounds, blocking active sites). Hence the global switch to UNLEADED PETROL from the 1970s onwards.`]},{type:`h`,text:`The lean-burn engine`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Standard ("stoichiometric") petrol engine: air/fuel ratio λ ≈ 1.0. Produces some unburnt hydrocarbons (rich pockets) and some NOx (hot zones) — handled by 3-way cat.`,`Lean-burn engine: λ > 1 (excess air). Lower combustion temperature → much less thermal NOx formed. More complete combustion → less unburnt hydrocarbon and CO.`,`Fuel economy benefit: leaner combustion = more oxygen-rich = more efficient extraction of energy from the fuel; up to 20% better fuel economy.`,`Drawback: with so much excess O₂ in the exhaust, the 3-way reduction of NOx no longer works (O₂ takes the catalyst sites and prevents NO reduction). Lean-burn engines need a DIFFERENT NOx control system: lean NOx trap (LNT) or selective catalytic reduction (SCR).`]},{type:`h`,text:`SCR — selective catalytic reduction (modern diesels)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Diesel engines run very lean (λ ≈ 1.5–2.5). Three-way catalysts cannot handle the NOx.`,`SCR: inject a urea solution (AdBlue / DEF — 32.5% urea in deionised water) into the hot exhaust. Urea decomposes to ammonia: (NH₂)₂CO + H₂O → 2NH₃ + CO₂.`,`Ammonia reduces NOx selectively over a copper-zeolite or vanadium-based catalyst: 4NH₃ + 4NO + O₂ → 4N₂ + 6H₂O. (The "selectivity" — SCR — is that NH₃ reduces NOx but does not waste reductant on the abundant O₂.)`,`Removal: 80–95% of NOx.`,`Drawback: requires AdBlue refilling (typical car: every 15 000–20 000 km). Modern diesels essentially all use SCR.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Why catalytic converters changed everything`,body:`Without catalytic converters, every car would emit ~10× the pollution it does today. The technology was mandated in the US (1975) and EU (1993), and is responsible for the dramatic improvement in urban air quality in industrialised countries despite the rise in vehicle numbers.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`3-way catalytic converter: Pt, Pd, Rh on ceramic honeycomb; oxidises CO + HC, reduces NOx.`,`Critical reaction: 2NO + 2CO → N₂ + 2CO₂ (Rh catalyst).`,`Lean-burn engine: better fuel economy + lower NOx but needs SCR (urea injection) for NOx control.`,`Lead poisons Pt — universal switch to unleaded fuel.`]}]},{id:`ev6`,title:`Power Generation & Climate`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Electricity generation accounts for about a quarter of all greenhouse gas emissions. The choice of fuel — coal, gas, nuclear, hydroelectric, or renewable — determines the chemistry of the emissions and therefore the environmental cost. Each source has trade-offs that the chemistry and the policy must address.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`POWER SOURCES · COMPARISON`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[`Source`,`Operational emissions`,`Key issue`],rows:[[`Coal`,`CO₂ + SO₂ + NOx`,`highest air pollution`],[`Oil`,`CO₂ + NOx`,`transport-linked`],[`Natural gas`,`CO₂`,`lower CO₂ than coal`],[`Nuclear`,`very low CO₂`,`radioactive waste / accidents`],[`Hydro`,`low CO₂`,`ecosystem / reservoir impacts`],[`Wind`,`none in operation`,`intermittent`],[`Solar PV`,`none in operation`,`manufacturing / intermittency`],[`Geothermal`,`low CO₂`,`location-limited`]],colWidths:[82,105,113],rowH:22}],subtitle:`Table shows operational emissions only.`},caption:`Comparison of major power sources`},{type:`h`,text:`Fossil fuels — combustion chemistry recap`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Coal: a complex, high-carbon, sulphur-containing solid. Composition varies but typically 70-90% C, 4-6% H, 1-4% S, 1-5% N, 5-15% O, plus mineral matter (the ash).`,`Combustion: C + O₂ → CO₂ (the dominant reaction). Roughly 3.7 kg of CO₂ per kg of carbon burnt. A 1 GW coal station emits ~6 million tonnes CO₂/year.`,`Side reactions: S + O₂ → SO₂ (chapter 2); 2N₂ + O₂ → 2NO (chapter 4); incomplete combustion → CO and unburnt carbon (soot).`,`Natural gas: mostly methane CH₄. CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O. About 0.55 kg CO₂ per kWh — half of coal's 1.0 kg CO₂/kWh because (i) more H per C (so more H₂O than CO₂) and (ii) higher conversion efficiency.`,`Oil: liquid hydrocarbons, intermediate. Mainly used for transport, not power generation (transport accounts for most oil use).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Climate change — the chemistry`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Greenhouse gases (CO₂, CH₄, N₂O, water vapour, fluorinated gases) absorb infrared radiation emitted by Earth's surface, trapping heat in the lower atmosphere.`,`CO₂ is the dominant anthropogenic greenhouse gas — long-lived (centuries-millennia in the atmosphere) and rising rapidly with fossil fuel use.`,`Pre-industrial atmospheric CO₂: 280 ppm. Today: ~420 ppm. The fastest known rise in geological history.`,`Effects: global mean temperature has risen ~1.2 °C since 1900. Heatwaves, sea level rise, more intense storms, ocean acidification, biodiversity loss.`,`Important to distinguish: traditional air pollutants (SO₂, NOx, CO) cause LOCAL/REGIONAL damage and are removed from the atmosphere on timescales of days. CO₂ causes GLOBAL damage and persists for centuries.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Decarbonising power — alternatives`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Nuclear: no CO₂ in operation; fuel (uranium) abundant; capacity factor ~90% (very reliable). Drawbacks: long-lived waste, accident risk, capital cost, public perception. (Chapter 7 covers the chemistry in detail.)`,`Hydroelectric: dam a river; turn turbines with falling water. Effectively zero CO₂ in operation, very long lifetime. Drawback: methane from rotting vegetation in the reservoir; ecological impact on river systems.`,`Wind: Pelton-type or modern horizontal-axis turbines. Zero CO₂ in operation. Intermittent (~25-40% capacity factor); needs storage or backup.`,`Solar PV: photons excite electrons in silicon (or thin-film) cells; current flows directly. Zero CO₂ in operation. Capital intensive; falling rapidly in cost.`,`Geothermal: hot water from underground rock; very limited geographic availability.`,`Biomass: in principle CO₂-neutral (plants absorb same CO₂ they release on combustion), but real life is messier (transport, processing, land use).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Energy storage — the key chemistry challenge`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Renewables are intermittent — wind doesn't blow at night, sun doesn't shine at night. Need to store energy from peak production for when it's needed.`,`Lithium-ion batteries: now the dominant grid-scale storage. Chemistry covered in PC-06 electrochemistry and AC-21 transition metals.`,`Pumped hydro: pump water uphill when energy is cheap; let it flow down through turbines when needed. Mature, large-scale.`,`Hydrogen: produce by electrolysis of water (2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂); store; burn or use in fuel cell to recover energy. Potential for "green" hydrogen if powered by renewables.`,`Compressed air, flywheels, thermal storage: niche but growing.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why this is a hard problem`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Coal and gas are CHEAP — fossil fuel prices remain competitive without carbon pricing.`,`Existing infrastructure (power lines, plants, networks) was built for steady fossil generation.`,`Renewables need new infrastructure (transmission lines, storage, demand-response).`,`Different countries have different resources (much wind in UK; much sun in southern Europe; much hydro in Scandinavia; coal in China and India).`,`Pace of transition is constrained by replacement rate of large infrastructure (typical lifespan 30-50 years).`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`CO₂ vs traditional pollutants`,body:`The chemistry of pollution control treats SO₂, NOx, particulates, and heavy metals — local-to-regional pollutants. Climate change is a different problem: CO₂ is not "captured" by an end-of-pipe control. The only real solutions are to burn less fossil fuel, capture CO₂ at source (CCS), or remove it from the atmosphere (not yet at scale).`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Coal: high CO₂, high SO₂, high NOx. Worst conventional fuel.`,`Gas: half the CO₂ of coal; very low SO₂; moderate NOx.`,`Nuclear: no CO₂ in operation; waste and accident concerns.`,`Wind/solar: zero CO₂ in operation; intermittent; needs storage.`,`Decarbonisation = the central environmental chemistry challenge of our time.`]}]},{id:`ev7`,title:`Nuclear Power & Hazards`,minutes:6,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Nuclear power generates electricity from the heat released by controlled fission of heavy nuclei (mostly ²³⁵U or ²³⁹Pu). It produces zero CO₂ in operation and supplies about 10% of global electricity. The hazards are entirely different from those of fossil fuel: instead of air pollutants, the issue is radioactive waste, accident risk, and naturally occurring radioactivity from uranium-bearing rocks.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`NUCLEAR · HAZARDS & CONTROL`,blocks:[{type:`cards`,cards:[{title:`Natural radon`,color:`primary`,body:[`Rn-222 from U-bearing rock`,`α-emitter`,`ventilation reduces indoor risk`]},{title:`Fuel cycle`,color:`accent`,body:[`fission products / spent fuel`,`some very long half-lives`,`shielding + geological storage`]},{title:`Accidents`,color:`pink`,body:[`loss of containment`,`radioisotope release`,`long-term land contamination`]}]}],subtitle:`High-level waste: vitrify, then deep-store.`},caption:`Three categories of nuclear hazard`},{type:`h`,text:`How nuclear power works (briefly)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Fuel: uranium, mined as the oxide U₃O₈ ("yellowcake"); enriched in the fissile isotope ²³⁵U from 0.7% (natural) to about 4–5% (reactor-grade); fabricated into ceramic UO₂ pellets stacked in zirconium-alloy fuel rods.`,`Fission: ²³⁵U + n → ²³⁶U* → fission fragments (e.g. ¹⁴⁰Ba + ⁹³Kr or many other combinations) + 2-3 n + ~200 MeV energy.`,`Each fission releases neutrons that can cause more fissions — chain reaction. Controlled by neutron-absorbing rods (B, Cd, Hf) inserted into the core.`,`Heat → boils water → steam turbine → electricity (just like a coal plant, but the heat source is fission, not combustion).`,`A 1 GW reactor consumes about 30 tonnes of fuel/year (vs ~3 million tonnes of coal — 100 000 times less mass).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Naturally occurring radioactivity`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Uranium and thorium are present at trace levels in most rocks, especially granite. They decay through long chains, releasing α-particles, β-particles, and γ-rays.`,`RADON (²²²Rn) is the most important hazard: a noble gas released from decay of U in rocks, diffuses through soil into buildings, accumulates indoors. α-emitter; if breathed in, lung cancer risk is significant.`,`Mitigation: ventilate basements; build with radon-impermeable membranes in high-Rn areas (e.g. Cornwall in the UK; parts of Pennsylvania in US).`,`Mining workers face higher exposure: U mining historically had very poor radiation protection; high lung cancer rates among uranium miners in the 1950s-70s prompted modern regulations.`,`Building stone: granite countertops emit some Rn but at safe levels for normal use.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Operational waste`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Low-level waste: contaminated tools, clothing, water; short half-lives (years); can be stored in shallow burial after a few decades.`,`Intermediate-level waste: reactor components, sludges; longer-lived; usually solidified in cement and stored in lined vaults.`,`High-level waste (the spent fuel): hot, intensely radioactive; contains fission products and trans-uranic elements.`,`Most worrying isotopes: ¹³⁷Cs (t½ 30 yr), ⁹⁰Sr (t½ 29 yr), ¹³¹I (t½ 8 days), and very long-lived actinides ²³⁹Pu (t½ 24 000 yr), ⁹⁹Tc (t½ 211 000 yr).`,`Volume is small — total global high-level waste from 60 years of nuclear power fits in a sports stadium. But it must be isolated from the biosphere for ~10⁵ years.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Disposal strategies`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Vitrification: incorporate waste into glass blocks (silicate-based) that resist leaching for 10⁵ years.`,`Deep geological repository: bury vitrified waste 500-1000 m underground in stable rock formations. Finland's Onkalo (operating from 2025) is the first; others in development in Sweden, France, Switzerland.`,`Reprocessing: chemically separate unburnt U and Pu from the waste, recycle as fresh fuel (closes the fuel cycle, reduces waste volume). Done in France, UK, Russia, Japan; not in US for proliferation concerns.`,`Transmutation: in principle, neutron irradiation could turn long-lived actinides into shorter-lived ones. Research-stage only.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Accidents`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Three Mile Island (1979, USA): partial core melt, but containment held. No measurable health effects on public.`,`Chernobyl (1986, USSR): graphite-moderated reactor went prompt-supercritical during a poorly-conducted test, exploded, fire spread fission products across Europe. Estimated 4 000 - 60 000 cancer deaths over decades.`,`Fukushima (2011, Japan): tsunami knocked out backup cooling at three reactors; cores melted; controlled venting and seawater quenching limited release. Estimated long-term cancer deaths ~ hundred or fewer.`,`Lessons: containment is critical; passive (failure-tolerant) cooling systems; siting away from major hazards.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`The nuclear paradox`,body:`Nuclear power is among the LEAST carbon-emitting and SAFEST per unit of energy produced (deaths per TWh) of any electricity source — including wind. Yet public perception emphasises rare accidents over the steady, long-term mortality from coal pollution. The chemistry is well understood; the politics is not.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Nuclear: zero CO₂ operation; fission of ²³⁵U/²³⁹Pu → heat → electricity.`,`Hazards: radon from U-bearing rocks; spent fuel; rare accidents.`,`Waste: ~10⁵ yr isolation needed; vitrification + deep geological repository.`,`Accidents: Three Mile Island (contained), Chernobyl (worst), Fukushima (moderate).`]}]},{id:`ev8`,title:`Waste Management Hierarchy`,minutes:5,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Modern waste management follows a five-step hierarchy, in order of preference: REDUCE, REUSE, RECYCLE, INCINERATE (with energy recovery), LANDFILL. The first three have lowest environmental impact; the last two are end-of-pipe options. Chemistry plays a role at every level — designing materials to be recyclable, optimising recycling processes, controlling combustion to minimise toxic emissions.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`WASTE MANAGEMENT · HIERARCHY`,blocks:[{type:`pyramid`,tiers:[{label:`REDUCE · best`,width:.35,color:`green`},{label:`REUSE`,width:.48,color:`primary`},{label:`RECYCLE`,width:.62,color:`accent`},{label:`INCINERATE / RECOVER ENERGY`,width:.78,color:`pink`},{label:`LANDFILL · last resort`,width:.95,color:`grey`}]},{type:`note`,text:`Prevent waste first; disposal is last.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Five-tier waste hierarchy — top is best`},{type:`h`,text:`Reduce`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Use less material in the first place. e.g. lighter packaging, dematerialised products, repairable rather than disposable.`,`Best for the environment but hardest to legislate or measure.`,`Examples: thinner aluminium drink cans (reduced by 40% over 30 years); EU bans on single-use plastic items; redesign of products for durability and repair.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Reuse`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Same physical item used multiple times. Glass milk bottles before disposable plastics; refillable water bottles; refurbished computers.`,`Energy savings vs new: typically 70-90% (depending on product).`,`Example: deposit-return schemes for glass and PET bottles. Currently ~95% return rate in countries that have them (Norway, Germany).`]},{type:`h`,text:`Recycle`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Collect waste material; reprocess it into raw material; manufacture new products from it.`,`Chemistry-intensive: paper de-inked; aluminium re-melted (saves 95% of energy vs primary smelt); steel re-melted; PET hydrolysed or re-melted; glass melted; lead-acid batteries decomposed and rebuilt.`,`Recycling rates: aluminium > 70%, paper > 60%, glass > 70% in EU. PET ~40%; mixed plastics much lower.`,`Single-stream "comingled" recycling improves consumer participation but increases contamination — sorting becomes harder.`,`Some materials cannot be recycled cost-effectively (low value, contamination, mixed materials) — these end up incinerated or landfilled.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Recovery (incineration with energy)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Burn at controlled temperature (typically > 850 °C to destroy organic toxins like dioxins).`,`Generate steam → electricity + district heating.`,`Reduces volume by ~90% (dense ash residue).`,`Issues: COMBUSTION PRODUCTS — CO₂, NOx, particulates, possibly dioxins (esp. from chlorinated plastics like PVC), SO₂ if S-containing waste, heavy metals from battery and other contamination.`,`Modern incinerators have advanced flue gas treatment: particulate filters (electrostatic precipitators, baghouses), SCR for NOx, scrubbers for SO₂ and HCl, activated carbon injection for dioxins and Hg.`,`Bottom ash often used as construction aggregate; fly ash hazardous, sent to special landfill.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Disposal (landfill)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Last resort. Put the waste in the ground, cover, monitor.`,`Modern engineered landfill: lined with HDPE membrane + clay to prevent leachate escape; collection pipes for leachate (which is treated); methane capture (anaerobic decomposition produces CH₄, a strong greenhouse gas if vented; usually flared to CO₂ or used for power).`,`Decomposition timescales: organic waste decomposes over decades; plastics over centuries; some materials effectively indefinite.`,`Issues: land use; leachate even with liners eventually fails; methane escapes; potential for groundwater contamination.`,`Old, uncontrolled landfills are environmental disasters — many require remediation decades after closure.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Material design matters`,body:`A product designed for easy recycling — separable components, single-material packaging, no glued mixed-material parts — recycles efficiently. A product not designed for recycling often cannot be recycled economically. Chemistry and engineering choices made at design time determine end-of-life options.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Hierarchy: reduce > reuse > recycle > recover (incinerate) > dispose (landfill).`,`Reduce: less material used. Reuse: same item, multiple lives. Recycle: reprocess to raw material.`,`Energy savings of recycling: aluminium saves 95% vs primary; steel ~70%; glass ~30%.`,`Modern landfills and incinerators have strong environmental controls but are not zero-impact.`]}]},{id:`ev9`,title:`Landfill, Incineration & Dumping`,minutes:6,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`When reduce, reuse and recycle have done their work, the residual waste needs disposal. Two engineering approaches dominate: landfill (controlled burial) and incineration (controlled combustion). A third, much less acceptable, is dumping at sea or in rivers. Each has specific chemistry that determines its environmental impact.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`LANDFILL vs INCINERATION`,blocks:[{type:`compare`,left:{title:`LANDFILL`,color:`primary`,body:[`+ cheap / established`,`− land use + CH₄`,`− leachate / slow decay`,`modern: liners + gas capture`]},right:{title:`INCINERATION`,color:`accent`,body:[`+ ~90% volume reduction`,`+ possible energy recovery`,`− CO₂ + ash`,`dioxins if poorly controlled`]}},{type:`note`,text:`Safe incineration needs high T + gas cleanup.`,emphasis:!0}]},caption:`Pros and cons of landfill vs incineration`},{type:`h`,text:`Modern engineered landfill — chemistry`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Liner: HDPE membrane + compacted clay layer at the base, to prevent leachate escape.`,`Cover: daily soil layer to control odours and pests; final cap when site is full (impermeable layer + soil + vegetation).`,`Leachate: water that percolates through the waste, dissolving soluble contaminants. Composition: high in BOD/COD, ammonia, heavy metals, organic chemicals. Collected by drainage pipes; treated like sewage before discharge.`,`Gas: anaerobic decomposition of organic waste produces CH₄ + CO₂ (50:50 mixture). Methane is captured via vertical wells, then either flared to CO₂ (less harmful greenhouse gas) or used as fuel for power generation.`,`Decomposition stages: aerobic (initial weeks) → anaerobic (years to decades). After 30-50 years a landfill is biologically "stable" but plastic and metals remain unchanged.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Modern incineration — chemistry`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Combustion conditions: > 850 °C, residence time 2 seconds, excess oxygen — to destroy organic toxins.`,`Primary combustion: organic waste + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O + heat. Inorganic (metals, ash) does not burn — it falls through as bottom ash.`,`Side products: NOx (from N in protein-rich waste; controlled by SCR or low-NOx burners); SO₂ (from S in waste; controlled by scrubbing); HCl (from PVC; scrubbing with NaOH); particulate matter (controlled by ESP/baghouse).`,`DIOXINS: a major historical issue. Dioxins (polychlorinated dibenzo-p-dioxins, PCDDs) form at incinerator temperatures of 200-400 °C from chlorine-containing organics. At > 850 °C they are destroyed. Modern incinerators prevent dioxin re-formation by RAPID quench from > 850 °C through the danger zone.`,`Activated carbon: injected before the final filter to adsorb residual dioxins, mercury, and trace organics.`,`Ash: bottom ash (~25% of input mass) often used as construction aggregate; fly ash (5%, from filters) is more hazardous and sent to special landfill.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Dumping in waterways and oceans`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Direct discharge of untreated sewage into rivers and seas: still common in many parts of the world.`,`Sewage chemistry: high in nutrients (NH₄⁺, NO₃⁻, PO₄³⁻), pathogens, organics. Effects: oxygen depletion (eutrophication), algal blooms, fish kills, disease.`,`Modern sewage treatment: 3-stage process — primary (settling), secondary (biological breakdown by bacteria), tertiary (nitrate/phosphate removal, UV/Cl disinfection).`,`Oil spills: major chemical impact on marine ecosystems. Crude oil floats and forms slicks; aromatic compounds dissolve into water; PAHs accumulate in shellfish; tar adheres to feathers/fur.`,`Cleanup: booms (containment), skimmers (recovery), dispersants (chemical break-up), bioremediation (oil-eating bacteria added). Each has trade-offs.`,`International law: MARPOL Convention regulates ship discharges; Ocean Dumping Convention bans dumping at sea of certain wastes (radioactive, chlorinated organic, mercury).`]},{type:`h`,text:`The "polluter pays" principle`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Modern environmental legislation in most countries: the producer of pollution bears the cost of cleanup or prevention.`,`Implementation: emission permits, pollution taxes, deposit-return schemes, "extended producer responsibility" (e.g. for packaging, electronics).`,`Effectiveness: variable. Where enforced (EU, US), pollution levels have fallen. Where weakly enforced, dumping persists.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`The right disposal for the right waste`,body:`Organic-rich biodegradable waste → composting > landfill. Recyclable materials → recycle, never landfill. Hazardous chemicals → specialist hazardous-waste landfill. Toxic combustibles → high-T incineration. Inert non-recyclable → engineered landfill. Sewage → biological treatment, never untreated discharge. Mixing categories increases all of the problems.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Modern landfill: lined, covered, leachate collected, methane captured.`,`Modern incineration: > 850 °C destroys dioxins; flue gas treatment controls NOx, SO₂, HCl, particulates.`,`Untreated sewage discharge: eutrophication, fish kills, disease.`,`Polluter-pays principle: producer bears cost of cleanup.`]}]},{id:`ev10`,title:`Heavy Metals & Industrial Effluent`,minutes:6,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Heavy metals — lead, mercury, cadmium, chromium, arsenic — are persistent toxic pollutants with no biological function in humans. Once released to the environment, they cycle indefinitely, biomagnify up food chains, and cause neurological, kidney, and other organ damage. Their chemistry of pollution control connects directly to the ion-exchange and chromatography of AC-22, and to the redox of AC-21.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`HEAVY METAL POLLUTION`,blocks:[{type:`table`,headers:[`Metal`,`Common source`,`Main hazard`],rows:[[`Pb`,`old paint · batteries · past fuel`,`neurotoxicity`],[`Hg`,`gold mining · coal`,`methylmercury · neurological`],[`Cd`,`batteries · ores`,`kidney damage`],[`Cr(VI)`,`tanning · plating`,`carcinogenic`],[`As`,`groundwater · mining`,`cancer · skin lesions`]],colWidths:[65,120,115],rowH:25}],subtitle:`Heavy metals persist and can biomagnify.`},caption:`Five major heavy-metal pollutants`},{type:`h`,text:`Lead — the historical legend`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Source: leaded petrol (banned in most countries by 2000), lead-based paint (banned in most rich countries by 1970s but still in older buildings), lead solder in plumbing, electronic waste, lead-acid batteries.`,`Toxicity: neurotoxin — IQ loss in children (3-5 IQ points per 10 µg/dL blood lead); cardiovascular disease in adults; reproductive effects.`,`Chemistry of removal: precipitation as PbS or PbCO₃ in lake sediments; sorption onto soil organic matter; ion-exchange resins for water treatment.`,`Notable success: leaded petrol phase-out cut atmospheric lead by ~95% in 30 years. Children's blood lead levels in industrialised countries fell similarly.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Mercury — methylmercury and bioaccumulation`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Sources: coal combustion (releases trace Hg from coal); artisanal gold mining (Hg used to amalgamate gold, then evaporated, releasing Hg vapour); industrial cell-room (chlor-alkali); past use in dental amalgam; mercury thermometers and switches.`,`Inorganic Hg(0) and Hg²⁺ are toxic but partly excreted. The dangerous form is METHYLMERCURY CH₃Hg⁺ — formed by anaerobic bacteria in lake sediments from inorganic Hg.`,`Methylmercury bioaccumulates: small fish ingest it, large fish eat many small fish, etc. Apex predators (tuna, swordfish, sharks) carry the highest concentrations.`,`Toxicity: methylmercury crosses the blood-brain barrier; severe neurological damage (Minamata disease, Japan, 1950s — fishing village poisoned by industrial Hg discharge into Minamata Bay).`,`Chemistry of removal: stack-gas treatment with activated carbon (adsorbs Hg) or selenium (forms HgSe, insoluble); avoid coal combustion; ban Hg in industrial processes.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Chromium — Cr(III) vs Cr(VI)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Different oxidation states have very different toxicities (link to AC-21 transition elements).`,`Cr(III) (e.g. Cr₂O₃): essentially non-toxic; even an essential trace element for glucose metabolism.`,`Cr(VI) (e.g. CrO₄²⁻, Cr₂O₇²⁻): highly toxic, carcinogenic, mutagenic. Penetrates cell membranes; reduces inside the cell to Cr(III) which damages DNA.`,`Sources of Cr(VI) pollution: leather tanning, electroplating, stainless-steel production, wood preservatives.`,`Treatment: REDUCE Cr(VI) to Cr(III) using SO₂ or FeSO₄, then precipitate Cr(III) as Cr(OH)₃ insoluble solid.`,`The Erin Brockovich case (California, 1990s) involved Cr(VI) contamination of groundwater.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Cadmium — batteries and ore`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Source: NiCd batteries (now mostly replaced by NiMH and Li-ion); zinc-ore smelting (Cd is a co-product of Zn).`,`Toxicity: kidney damage (the Itai-Itai disease in Japan, 1910s-1960s, was Cd poisoning from contaminated rice); bone weakness; carcinogen (lung).`,`Treatment: precipitation as Cd(OH)₂ or CdS; recovery from battery waste streams.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Arsenic — natural and anthropogenic`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Largely a NATURAL contaminant: dissolves from arsenic-bearing rocks into groundwater. Worst affected: Bangladesh (10s of millions exposed), West Bengal, parts of Argentina, Vietnam.`,`Anthropogenic: tin and copper smelting; pesticides (now banned); wood preservatives.`,`Toxicity: chronic — skin lesions (arsenicosis); cancer (lung, bladder, skin); cardiovascular.`,`Treatment: oxidise As(III) to As(V); precipitate with iron(III) salts (forms FeAsO₄ insoluble); also activated alumina sorption.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Industrial waste-water treatment using ion-exchange`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Waste water from electroplating, mining, textile dyeing contains mixed heavy-metal cations.`,`Ion-exchange resin (chelating type, with iminodiacetate or aminophosphonate groups) selectively binds Cu²⁺, Pb²⁺, Cd²⁺, Hg²⁺ in preference to Na⁺ and Ca²⁺.`,`Flow waste through the column. Metals are retained on the resin. Effluent is metal-free.`,`Regenerate the resin with concentrated acid: acid releases the metals as soluble salts (concentrated waste stream, much smaller volume — easier to handle).`,`Metal recovery: precipitate from the regeneration acid as metal hydroxides; sometimes electroplate back to recover pure metal.`,`Same chemistry as water softening (chapter 7 of AC-22) but with chelating resins for heavy metals.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Mine waste`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Tailings: ground rock left after the metal has been extracted; often contains residual metal and toxic chemicals from extraction.`,`Acid mine drainage: pyrite (FeS₂) in tailings oxidises in air to give H₂SO₄ + Fe³⁺. The acidic water (often pH 2-4) leaches more heavy metals from the rock.`,`4FeS₂ + 15O₂ + 14H₂O → 4Fe(OH)₃ + 8H₂SO₄.`,`Treatment: limestone neutralises acid (CaCO₃ + H₂SO₄ → CaSO₄ + CO₂ + H₂O); precipitates Fe(OH)₃; constructed wetlands use sulphate-reducing bacteria to immobilise residual metals as sulphides.`,`Many old, abandoned mines continue to discharge for centuries.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Heavy metals never go away`,body:`Unlike organic pollutants (which can be broken down by microbes or sunlight), heavy metals are ELEMENTS — they cannot be destroyed. They can only be moved or immobilised. Pollution prevention is therefore far more cost-effective than cleanup.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Heavy metals (Pb, Hg, Cd, Cr, As) are persistent, toxic, bioaccumulate.`,`Lead: phase-out of leaded petrol cut atmospheric Pb by 95%.`,`Mercury: methylmercury bioaccumulates up food chain; coal main source.`,`Chromium: Cr(VI) toxic; reduce to Cr(III) and precipitate as hydroxide.`,`Industrial waste-water: ion-exchange recovers metals selectively.`,`Acid mine drainage: pyrite oxidation; treated with limestone neutralisation.`]}]}],quizzes:[{q:`The main acid in acid rain is:`,opts:[`HCl`,`H₂SO₄`,`HF`,`HNO₃ only`],ans:1,hint:`From SO₂ → SO₃ → H₂SO₄ in the atmosphere.`},{q:`SO₂ in flue gas comes from:`,opts:[`N in air`,`S in coal/oil`,`C in coal`,`Water vapour`],ans:1,hint:`Coal contains 1-4% S as pyrite or organic S.`},{q:`Acid rain damages limestone buildings via:`,opts:[`CaCO₃ + H₂SO₄ → CaSO₄ + CO₂ + H₂O`,`Photolysis`,`Oxidation`,`Reduction`],ans:0,hint:`Carbonate dissolves in acid; H₂O and CO₂ released.`},{q:`Flue gas desulphurisation typically uses:`,opts:[`NaOH`,`CaCO₃ slurry`,`KMnO₄`,`Vacuum`],ans:1,hint:`Limestone slurry; produces gypsum.`},{q:`NOx forms in vehicle engines because:`,opts:[`Fuel contains N`,`High T (>1500 °C) drives N₂ + O₂ → 2NO`,`Catalyst makes it`,`Air contains it already`],ans:1,hint:`Thermal NOx from atmospheric N₂.`},{q:`In a 3-way catalytic converter, NOx is:`,opts:[`Oxidised to NO₃⁻`,`Reduced to N₂ (by CO over Rh)`,`Adsorbed on Pt`,`Filtered`],ans:1,hint:`2NO + 2CO → N₂ + 2CO₂; rhodium catalyst.`},{q:`Catalytic converters are destroyed by:`,opts:[`Cold weather`,`Lead (Pb poisons Pt)`,`Vibration`,`Salt spray`],ans:1,hint:`Pb forms stable Pt-Pb compounds; needs unleaded fuel.`},{q:`Lean-burn engines reduce NOx because:`,opts:[`Excess air → lower combustion T`,`Higher T`,`No oxygen`,`Catalyst`],ans:0,hint:`λ > 1 means cooler combustion, less thermal NOx.`},{q:`Diesel engines need SCR (with urea) because:`,opts:[`Diesel has more N`,`Lean exhaust prevents 3-way reduction; SCR uses NH₃ to reduce NOx selectively`,`No O₂`,`Just for show`],ans:1,hint:`Excess O₂ in lean exhaust spoils 3-way reduction.`},{q:`Coal vs natural gas, comparing CO₂ per kWh of electricity:`,opts:[`Equal`,`Coal twice as much as gas`,`Gas twice as much as coal`,`Hundredfold difference`],ans:1,hint:`Coal ~1.0 kg/kWh; gas ~0.55 kg/kWh.`},{q:`Nuclear power produces:`,opts:[`Lots of CO₂`,`Zero CO₂ in operation`,`SO₂`,`NOx`],ans:1,hint:`Fission, not combustion.`},{q:`Radon hazard comes from:`,opts:[`Nuclear power plants`,`Decay of natural U in rocks (esp. granite)`,`Cosmic rays`,`Coal`],ans:1,hint:`Rn-222 from U-238 decay chain.`},{q:`High-level nuclear waste is typically immobilised by:`,opts:[`Burning`,`Vitrification (fusion into glass blocks)`,`Burying loose`,`Dumping at sea`],ans:1,hint:`Glass resists leaching for 10⁵ yr; then deep geological storage.`},{q:`The waste hierarchy from BEST to LAST RESORT is:`,opts:[`Landfill > recycle > reuse > reduce`,`Reduce > reuse > recycle > recover > dispose`,`Burn > bury > recycle`,`Reduce > bury > burn`],ans:1,hint:`Reduce first; landfill last.`},{q:`Burning PVC at low T (<800 °C) is dangerous because:`,opts:[`It does not burn`,`Releases HCl + dioxins`,`Releases NOx`,`Produces CO₂`],ans:1,hint:`PVC contains Cl; dioxins form at 200-400 °C window.`},{q:`Modern incinerators destroy dioxins by:`,opts:[`Adding NaOH`,`Operating > 850 °C with rapid quench through 200-400 °C zone`,`Filtering`,`Cooling slowly`],ans:1,hint:`> 850 °C destroys dioxins; rapid quench prevents re-formation.`},{q:`Methane production in landfills is:`,opts:[`Aerobic oxidation`,`Anaerobic decomposition of organic waste`,`A safety feature`,`Negligible`],ans:1,hint:`Anaerobic conditions in deep waste → CH₄ + CO₂.`},{q:`Modern landfills control methane by:`,opts:[`Releasing it`,`Capture and either flare to CO₂ or use for power`,`Converting to LPG`,`Dissolving in water`],ans:1,hint:`Vertical wells capture; flaring at minimum, energy generation preferable.`},{q:`The most toxic form of mercury for bioaccumulation is:`,opts:[`Hg(0) liquid`,`Inorganic Hg²⁺`,`Methylmercury CH₃Hg⁺`,`HgS solid`],ans:2,hint:`Methylmercury crosses the blood-brain barrier; biomagnifies up food chain.`},{q:`Chromium toxicity depends on oxidation state:`,opts:[`All Cr is equally toxic`,`Cr(III) safe; Cr(VI) carcinogenic`,`Cr(VI) safe; Cr(III) toxic`,`Only metallic Cr is toxic`],ans:1,hint:`Cr(III) is essential trace; Cr(VI) is highly toxic and carcinogenic.`},{q:`Cr(VI) is treated by:`,opts:[`Leaving it alone`,`Reducing to Cr(III) and precipitating as Cr(OH)₃`,`Oxidising to Cr(VII)`,`Burning`],ans:1,hint:`Reduce with SO₂ or FeSO₄, then alkaline precipitate.`},{q:`Lead pollution dropped dramatically after:`,opts:[`Use of catalytic converters`,`Phase-out of leaded petrol`,`Recycling of batteries`,`All of these`],ans:3,hint:`Phase-out alone cut atmospheric Pb by 95%; all three contributed.`},{q:`Acid mine drainage forms when:`,opts:[`Rain water dissolves rock`,`Pyrite (FeS₂) in tailings oxidises to give H₂SO₄ and Fe³⁺`,`Mining adds acid`,`Microbes produce acid`],ans:1,hint:`4FeS₂ + 15O₂ + 14H₂O → 4Fe(OH)₃ + 8H₂SO₄.`},{q:`Heavy metals can be removed from waste water by:`,opts:[`Boiling`,`Filtering`,`Ion-exchange resin (chelating type)`,`UV light`],ans:2,hint:`Same chemistry as water softening, with chelating resin selective for heavy metals.`},{q:`Eutrophication of rivers is caused by:`,opts:[`Heavy metals`,`Excess N and P (fertilisers, sewage)`,`Sediment only`,`Heat`],ans:1,hint:`Algal bloom; oxygen depletion; fish kill.`},{q:`Ground-level ozone (smog) forms by:`,opts:[`Direct emission`,`Reaction of NOx + VOCs in sunlight`,`Lightning only`,`Volcanic activity`],ans:1,hint:`Photochemical smog: NOx + hydrocarbons + UV → O₃ and PAN.`},{q:`CO₂ emissions are different from SO₂ pollution because:`,opts:[`CO₂ is more toxic`,`CO₂ is global and long-lived; SO₂ is regional and short-lived`,`CO₂ comes from cars only`,`No difference`],ans:1,hint:`CO₂: centuries in atmosphere, global mixing. SO₂: days, regional.`},{q:`The MAIN strategy to address climate change is:`,opts:[`Bigger filters`,`Reduce burning of fossil fuels`,`Plant more trees only`,`Capture all emissions`],ans:1,hint:`Decarbonising electricity, transport, industry; CO₂ has no end-of-pipe fix.`}],multi:[],structured:[{title:`SO₂ — formation and control`,marks:8,q:`Coal-fired power stations are a major source of SO₂ pollution.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Explain the chemistry by which SO₂ from a coal-fired plant becomes sulphuric acid in the atmosphere.`,marks:3,ans:`Coal contains 1–4% sulphur, present as iron pyrite FeS₂ and as organic sulphur. On combustion in the boiler, sulphur is oxidised: S + O₂ → SO₂. The SO₂ is released to the atmosphere through the chimney. In the atmosphere, SO₂ is further oxidised to sulphur trioxide: 2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃. This step is slow without catalysts but is accelerated by the presence of NOx (which acts as a catalytic oxygen carrier) and by sunlight. The SO₃ then dissolves in atmospheric water droplets to form sulphuric acid: SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄. Sulphuric acid is a strong acid (fully dissociated) so the rain becomes acidic. Some SO₂ also dissolves directly: SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃ (a weak acid contributing to the acidity).`},{q:`(b) Describe the operating principle of flue gas desulphurisation (FGD) using limestone and write the overall equation.`,marks:3,ans:`The flue gas, at about 150 °C, is passed through a chamber where it is sprayed with a slurry of finely ground calcium carbonate (CaCO₃) in water. The acidic SO₂ dissolves in the water and reacts with the alkaline CaCO₃. With the small amount of oxygen present, the sulphur is oxidised from S(IV) to S(VI), and the solid product is calcium sulphate (gypsum), which precipitates out. Overall equation: 2CaCO₃(s) + 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2CaSO₄(s) + 2CO₂(g). Removal efficiency is typically 90–98%. The gypsum (CaSO₄·2H₂O) is collected, dewatered and sold for plasterboard manufacture, turning a waste stream into a saleable by-product. Drawback: the process produces additional CO₂.`},{q:`(c) Compare wet limestone FGD with fluidised-bed combustion in terms of SO₂ removal and impact on NOx emissions.`,marks:2,ans:`Wet limestone FGD: a post-combustion treatment, with the flue gas reacting in a separate scrubber unit. Removes 90–98% of SO₂, with no significant effect on NOx (because the gas is already cool and NOx has already formed). FBC: in-situ capture during combustion. Coal is burnt in a bed of crushed limestone particles fluidised by upward air flow; SO₂ is captured as it forms by reaction with CaO (from CaCO₃ decomposition) to give CaSO₄. SO₂ removal is somewhat lower (70–90%). However, FBC operates at a much lower temperature (~850 °C vs ~1500 °C in pulverised-coal boilers), so thermal NOx formation is also greatly reduced — typically by 50–70%. So FBC has a co-benefit on NOx that wet FGD does not, but slightly lower SO₂ removal.`}]},{title:`NOx control`,marks:9,q:`Vehicles are major sources of nitrogen oxide pollution.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Explain how NOx forms in a petrol engine, even though petrol contains essentially no nitrogen.`,marks:3,ans:`NOx forms by THERMAL OXIDATION of atmospheric nitrogen during combustion. The fuel-air mixture in a petrol engine cylinder reaches peak temperatures of about 2500 °C in the milliseconds during combustion. At these high temperatures, the reaction N₂ + O₂ → 2NO becomes thermodynamically favourable and kinetically fast. (At room temperature the same reaction is essentially impossible — NO is endothermic by 90 kJ/mol — but at 2500 °C the equilibrium shifts and the rate becomes appreciable.) Once formed, the NO partially oxidises in the cooler parts of the exhaust: 2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂. The mixture of NO and NO₂ is collectively called NOx. Hence even though the fuel is essentially N-free, atmospheric N₂ provides the nitrogen and the high combustion temperature drives the reaction.`},{q:`(b) Describe the structure of a three-way catalytic converter and write equations for the reactions it catalyses.`,marks:4,ans:`Structure: a ceramic honeycomb (cordierite) is coated with a high-surface-area "washcoat" of γ-alumina onto which the precious metal catalysts platinum (Pt), palladium (Pd) and rhodium (Rh) are deposited as nanoparticles. The honeycomb design maximises the contact between exhaust gases and catalyst surface while keeping back-pressure low. The whole assembly is housed in a stainless steel canister and fitted in the exhaust system between engine and tailpipe. Reactions catalysed: (i) Oxidation of CO over Pt or Pd: 2CO + O₂ → 2CO₂. (ii) Oxidation of unburnt hydrocarbons: e.g. C₈H₁₈ + 12½O₂ → 8CO₂ + 9H₂O. (iii) Reduction of NOx by CO over Rh: 2NO + 2CO → N₂ + 2CO₂. The "three-way" refers to its handling of all three pollutant types (CO, hydrocarbons, NOx) simultaneously. Operating temperature must be above 350 °C; below this the catalyst is inactive (cold-start problem).`},{q:`(c) Explain why catalytic converters are destroyed by leaded petrol, and outline the alternative NOx-control method used in modern diesels.`,marks:2,ans:`Lead from leaded petrol forms stable Pt-Pb intermetallic compounds on the catalyst surface, blocking the active sites. The catalyst becomes permanently poisoned within a few hundred miles. This is why the introduction of catalytic converters (US 1975, EU 1993) was paired with mandatory unleaded petrol. Modern diesels: diesel engines run very lean (excess air, λ = 1.5–2.5), so the exhaust contains too much O₂ for the 3-way reduction of NOx to work. Instead, diesels use SELECTIVE CATALYTIC REDUCTION (SCR): inject AdBlue solution (32.5% urea in deionised water) into the hot exhaust. Urea decomposes to ammonia: (NH₂)₂CO + H₂O → 2NH₃ + CO₂. The ammonia then reduces NOx selectively over a copper-zeolite catalyst: 4NH₃ + 4NO + O₂ → 4N₂ + 6H₂O. Removal: 80–95% of NOx, even in the lean exhaust.`}]},{title:`Power generation and climate`,marks:7,q:`Different methods of generating electricity have different environmental impacts.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Compare coal-fired and gas-fired electricity generation in terms of CO₂ emissions per kWh, with brief chemistry to explain the difference.`,marks:3,ans:`Coal-fired plants typically emit ~1.0 kg of CO₂ per kWh of electricity. Gas-fired plants emit ~0.5 kg CO₂ per kWh — about half. Two reasons: (i) Chemistry: coal is mostly carbon (~80% C, ~5% H), so combustion gives mostly CO₂ and only a small amount of H₂O. Methane (the main component of natural gas) is CH₄ — combustion 2CH₄ + 4O₂ → 2CO₂ + 4H₂O. About half the energy comes from C → CO₂ and half from H → H₂O, so per unit of energy released, less CO₂ is produced than for the carbon-rich coal. (ii) Engineering: modern combined-cycle gas turbines (CCGT) achieve ~60% thermal efficiency vs ~38% for a typical coal plant, so for each kWh of electricity less fuel is burned. The two effects together give the ~50% reduction.`},{q:`(b) State TWO advantages and TWO disadvantages of nuclear power compared with fossil fuel.`,marks:2,ans:`Advantages: (1) Zero CO₂ emissions in operation — no greenhouse gas contribution from generation. (2) Very high energy density: ~30 tonnes of fuel per GW per year (vs ~3 million tonnes of coal); much smaller fuel logistics. Other accepted: (3) High capacity factor (~90%) — reliable baseload. (4) Energy security: long-term fuel supply from diverse sources. Disadvantages: (1) Long-lived radioactive waste requires isolation for ~10⁵ years; high cost of disposal. (2) Low-probability but high-consequence accident risk (Chernobyl, Fukushima); hard for the public to evaluate. Other accepted: (3) Capital cost high — large up-front investment. (4) Risk of nuclear weapons proliferation in the fuel cycle.`},{q:`(c) Why is reducing CO₂ emissions a different challenge from reducing SO₂ or NOx?`,marks:2,ans:`SO₂ and NOx are local-to-regional pollutants that can be REMOVED from flue gas by end-of-pipe treatments (FGD, catalytic converters, SCR). They have atmospheric lifetimes of days, so once emissions are cut, the atmospheric concentration falls quickly. CO₂ is different: (i) it has an atmospheric lifetime of CENTURIES, so reducing emissions today does not lower atmospheric CO₂ for many decades; (ii) its global concentration mixes uniformly, so emissions from any country contribute to global warming everywhere; (iii) there is no practical end-of-pipe scrubber for the volume of CO₂ produced (carbon capture and storage is an active research area but not yet at scale); (iv) the only realistic solutions are to BURN LESS fossil fuel (efficiency, electrification of transport, shift to renewables and nuclear) or to remove CO₂ from the atmosphere by direct air capture or reforestation. Sometimes summarised: SO₂ is a flow problem (turn off the tap and the bath empties); CO₂ is a stock problem (the bath is filling up faster than it drains).`}]},{title:`Waste management`,marks:8,q:`Modern waste management distinguishes among reduce, reuse, recycle, recover and dispose.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Explain the meaning of each tier of the waste hierarchy and why the hierarchy is in this order.`,marks:3,ans:`Reduce: use less material in the first place. Cut packaging, dematerialise, design for durability. Reuse: same item used multiple times before disposal. e.g. refillable bottles, repaired electronics, second-hand goods. Recycle: collect waste, reprocess into raw material, manufacture new products. e.g. melting aluminium cans, melting glass bottles, hydrolysing PET. Recover: extract value from waste that cannot be recycled, typically by INCINERATION with energy recovery (steam → electricity + district heating). Dispose: landfill — the last resort. The hierarchy reflects environmental impact (best at top) and resource economics. Reducing avoids both extraction of raw materials and waste creation. Reusing avoids the energy of recycling. Recycling saves more energy than producing virgin material (e.g. aluminium recycling saves 95% of primary smelting energy). Energy recovery extracts some value from non-recyclable material. Landfill recovers no value and may pose long-term environmental risk.`},{q:`(b) Modern incinerators must operate above 850 °C and rapidly cool the flue gas through the 200-400 °C range. Explain the chemistry behind these requirements.`,marks:3,ans:`Above 850 °C, organic chemicals — including chlorinated aromatics like dioxins (PCDDs) — are completely destroyed by oxidation. The high temperature ensures that any toxic organics in the waste are broken down to simple molecules (mainly CO₂, H₂O, HCl). Below this temperature, complete oxidation is slow and partial-combustion products survive. The 200-400 °C range is the DE NOVO SYNTHESIS window: dioxins can FORM from chlorine-containing carbon residues in the presence of catalytic transition metal salts in the fly ash. The reaction 2C + Cl₂ + ½O₂ → "PCDD" can occur on cooling fly ash particles at these temperatures. So if the gas is allowed to cool slowly through this range, dioxins re-form even though they were destroyed at the high temperature. Modern incinerators use a "rapid quench" — typically a spray of cold water — to bring the gas through the dangerous range in seconds rather than minutes, preventing significant dioxin re-formation. Activated carbon injection downstream then captures any residual dioxins, mercury, and trace organics.`},{q:`(c) State TWO advantages and TWO disadvantages of modern engineered landfill compared with high-temperature incineration.`,marks:2,ans:`Landfill advantages: (1) Cheaper per tonne of waste handled (about half the cost of modern incineration). (2) Lower energy and operational complexity — no high-T combustion, no advanced flue gas treatment. Landfill disadvantages: (1) Land use: a large landfill site lasts only ~30 years before being capped; new sites must be found constantly. (2) Long-term liability: leachate may eventually escape liners (HDPE membranes lifetime is < 100 years); methane release continues for decades; site monitoring required for ~30 years post-closure. Other acceptable answers: (3) Slow decomposition of plastics; (4) loss of materials that could have been recovered via recycling or energy; (5) groundwater contamination risk; (6) wildlife and pest issues; (7) odours.`}]},{title:`Heavy metal contamination`,marks:8,q:`Industrial waste water from a copper electroplating factory contains 200 ppm Cu²⁺, 50 ppm Cr(VI) (as CrO₄²⁻) and 10 ppm Pb²⁺.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Describe a chemical treatment to remove the chromium and explain the chemistry.`,marks:3,ans:`Cr(VI) is highly toxic and mobile; it must be REDUCED to Cr(III), which is much less toxic and easily precipitated. Step 1: Acidify the waste water to pH 2-3 using H₂SO₄. This converts CrO₄²⁻ to Cr₂O₇²⁻ (the active form) and prepares for the redox step. Step 2: Add a stoichiometric amount of a reducing agent — commonly SO₂(g) or Na₂S₂O₅ (sodium metabisulphite) or FeSO₄(aq). Reaction: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 3SO₂ + 2H⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 3SO₄²⁻ + H₂O. Step 3: Adjust the pH to ~9 with NaOH. The Cr³⁺ ions then precipitate as the insoluble hydroxide: Cr³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Cr(OH)₃(s). The solid Cr(OH)₃ is filtered off and disposed of as hazardous waste (or recovered for chromium recycling). Final effluent contains < 0.1 ppm Cr — well below the discharge limit.`},{q:`(b) Explain how ion-exchange chromatography can be used to remove the Cu²⁺ and Pb²⁺ from the same waste stream.`,marks:3,ans:`Use a CHELATING ion-exchange resin — a polymer carrying iminodiacetate or aminophosphonate groups that bind divalent transition-metal cations strongly through coordinate bonds (essentially the same chemistry as EDTA chelation). Pass the waste water through a column packed with this resin. The Cu²⁺ and Pb²⁺ bind to the chelating groups and are retained on the resin; Na⁺ and Ca²⁺ pass through (the chelating resin is selective for transition metals). Effluent from the column has heavy metal levels below 0.1 ppm. When the resin is exhausted (typically after thousands of bed volumes of waste), it is regenerated by passing concentrated H₂SO₄ down the column — the protons displace the bound metals: H₂SO₄ + (Resin–Cu²⁺) → Resin–H₂ + CuSO₄. The metals are now in concentrated acid (a much smaller volume than the original waste water) — typically 1000× concentrated. From this concentrate, copper can be recovered by electroplating (Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, deposited on a steel cathode); lead can be precipitated as PbSO₄ or Pb(OH)₂. The resin returns to service. The overall process turns dilute toxic waste into recovered metal + clean water.`},{q:`(c) Discuss the broader environmental significance of preventing rather than cleaning up heavy-metal pollution.`,marks:2,ans:`Heavy metals are ELEMENTS — they cannot be destroyed by any chemical or biological process. Pollution control can MOVE them (concentrate them in sludges or filters) but the metals continue to exist somewhere. Once heavy metals are dispersed across an ecosystem (in sediments, soils, groundwater) they cycle indefinitely and bioaccumulate in food chains. Cleanup of contaminated sites is enormously expensive — typically 10-100 times the cost of preventing the contamination at source. Examples: lead phase-out in petrol prevented cumulative emissions of millions of tonnes of Pb; the cost of cleaning up the same Pb from urban dust, paints, soils, and people's blood would be staggering. Therefore from an environmental and economic perspective, "polluter pays" + emission limits + product redesign (e.g. lead-free solder, Hg-free dental amalgam, Cr(III) tannery chemistry replacing Cr(VI)) are far more cost-effective than end-of-pipe cleanup. The Minamata Convention on Mercury (2013) and various electronic waste directives embody this principle.`}]}],worked:[{title:`Calculate SO₂ emissions from coal`,diff:`medium`,q:`A power station burns 5 × 10⁶ tonnes of coal per year. The coal contains 2.5% sulphur by mass. (a) Calculate the mass of SO₂ produced annually if no controls are fitted. (b) Calculate the mass of SO₂ released if FGD removes 95%.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Mass of S in coal = 5 × 10⁶ × 0.025 = 1.25 × 10⁵ tonnes/year = 1.25 × 10⁸ kg/year.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`All S burns: S + O₂ → SO₂. Stoichiometry 1:1 by moles.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Moles of S = 1.25 × 10⁸ / 32 = 3.91 × 10⁶ kmol. Mass of SO₂ = 3.91 × 10⁶ × 64 = 2.50 × 10⁸ kg = 2.5 × 10⁵ tonnes per year.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`(b) FGD removes 95%, so 5% (= 1.25 × 10⁴ tonnes) reaches the atmosphere.`}],ans:`(a) 250 000 tonnes SO₂/year uncontrolled. (b) 12 500 tonnes/year with 95% FGD. The reduction (~240 000 tonnes/yr) avoids approximately 350 000 tonnes of acid rain (H₂SO₄).`},{title:`CO₂ from a typical coal plant`,diff:`easy`,q:`Estimate the annual CO₂ emissions from a 1 GW coal-fired power plant operating at 30% thermal efficiency, with capacity factor 80%. Coal energy density 25 MJ/kg, carbon content 75%.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Annual electricity output = 1 GW × 0.80 × 8760 h = 7008 GWh = 2.52 × 10¹³ kJ.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Thermal input needed = output / efficiency = 2.52 × 10¹³ / 0.30 = 8.41 × 10¹³ kJ.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Mass of coal = 8.41 × 10¹³ kJ / 25 000 kJ kg⁻¹ = 3.36 × 10⁹ kg = 3.36 × 10⁶ tonnes per year.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Mass of carbon = 0.75 × 3.36 × 10⁶ = 2.52 × 10⁶ tonnes C.`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`C + O₂ → CO₂. Mass ratio CO₂/C = 44/12 = 3.67. So mass CO₂ = 2.52 × 10⁶ × 3.67 = 9.25 × 10⁶ tonnes per year.`}],ans:`Approximately 9.3 million tonnes of CO₂ per year. A typical 1 GW coal plant emits about the same CO₂ as 2 million cars.`},{title:`Reduction of Cr(VI) by sulphite`,diff:`medium`,q:`A waste stream contains 100 mg/L of Cr(VI) (as Cr₂O₇²⁻ in acidic solution). Calculate the minimum mass of sodium metabisulphite Na₂S₂O₅ needed per litre to reduce all the Cr(VI) to Cr(III). The active reductant is SO₂; one mole of Na₂S₂O₅ provides two moles of SO₂.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Concentration of Cr in solution = 100 mg/L = 0.100 g/L. Moles of Cr per L = 0.100 / 52 = 1.92 × 10⁻³ mol/L.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Reduction equation: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 3SO₂ + 2H⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 3SO₄²⁻ + H₂O. So 2 mol Cr requires 3 mol SO₂.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Moles of SO₂ needed per L = (3/2) × 1.92 × 10⁻³ = 2.88 × 10⁻³ mol.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Each mole of Na₂S₂O₅ (Mr = 190) gives 2 mol SO₂. Moles of Na₂S₂O₅ = 2.88 × 10⁻³ / 2 = 1.44 × 10⁻³ mol.`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`Mass = 1.44 × 10⁻³ × 190 = 0.274 g/L = 274 mg/L.`},{label:`STEP 6`,text:`In practice, a 10-20% excess is used to ensure complete reduction → ~330 mg/L.`}],ans:`Stoichiometric requirement: 274 mg Na₂S₂O₅ per litre. With a 20% excess for kinetic reasons: ~330 mg/L.`},{title:`Optimum incinerator temperature`,diff:`easy`,q:`A municipal incinerator must completely destroy dioxins. State the minimum operating temperature and explain why a rapid quench through 200-400 °C is also required.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Dioxins (PCDDs) are thermally labile above 850 °C — at this temperature complete oxidation to CO₂ + HCl + H₂O dominates.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`However, when the flue gas COOLS, the temperature passes back through the 200-400 °C range. In this window, dioxins can RE-FORM by "de novo synthesis" from chlorine-containing carbon residues in the fly ash, catalysed by transition metal salts on the ash particles.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`If cooling is slow, the gas spends minutes in this danger zone and significant amounts of dioxin reform.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Solution: rapid quench. Spray cold water into the flue gas to drop the temperature from 850 °C to below 200 °C in seconds. The de novo synthesis kinetics are too slow at this short residence time to give significant product.`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`Activated carbon is also injected upstream of the final particulate filter to adsorb any residual dioxins, mercury, and other trace organics.`}],ans:`Minimum operating temperature: 850 °C with residence time ≥ 2 seconds. Rapid quench through 200-400 °C prevents de novo reformation of dioxins. Activated carbon in flue gas treatment captures residuals.`},{title:`Choosing a power-source mix`,diff:`medium`,q:`A country wants to cut its electricity-sector CO₂ emissions by 80% within 20 years, while keeping electricity affordable. Outline a mix of strategies.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Assess the current portfolio: identify the highest-carbon plants (typically old coal stations) — these are the priority replacements.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Replace coal with gas in the short term (gas cuts CO₂ per kWh by 50% with existing technology). Cheap quick win.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Build out renewables — wind and solar are cheapest new generation in most markets. Targets 30-50% renewable share.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Add energy storage (batteries, pumped hydro, hydrogen) to handle the intermittency of wind/solar. ~10% of total capacity.`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`Maintain or expand nuclear: zero-CO₂ baseload that complements intermittent renewables. Slow to build but stable for decades.`},{label:`STEP 6`,text:`Improve transmission: high-voltage lines connect distant renewable resources (e.g. desert solar to cities; offshore wind to demand centres).`},{label:`STEP 7`,text:`Demand-side: efficiency improvements in buildings (LED lighting, heat pumps, insulation); smart grid for demand response.`},{label:`STEP 8`,text:`Carbon pricing: tax CO₂ emissions or cap-and-trade — internalises the climate cost; drives investment in low-carbon options.`}],ans:`A combined strategy: phase out coal, expand wind/solar/nuclear, build storage and transmission, improve efficiency on the demand side, and price carbon. No single technology can do it alone; the mix depends on local resources and economics.`}],tutorPrompts:[`Walk me through how SO₂ becomes acid rain`,`Compare wet FGD with fluidised-bed combustion`,`Explain catalytic converter chemistry`,`Why is CO₂ a different problem from SO₂?`,`Describe ion-exchange treatment of heavy metals`,`Outline the chemistry of acid mine drainage`]};export{e as ENVIRONMENT};